# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# check_hardware.conf - configuration for the IML hardware checks.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Copy this file to check_hardware.conf and edit. It is sourced as shell, so
# it can redefine anything the scripts define - including functions, which is
# the escape hatch for a site-specific quirk that does not deserve a flag.
#
#   cp check_hardware.conf.example check_hardware.conf
#
# PRECEDENCE: command line  >  this file  >  built-in defaults.
# The scripts parse their arguments, source this file, then re-apply the
# arguments, so a flag always beats a setting here. Nothing in this file can
# override something you typed.
#
# All three IML scripts read this same file:
#   check_iml/check_hpe_iml.sh      one HPE host
#   check_iml/check_lenovo_iml.sh   one Lenovo host
#   check_hardware.sh               the whole fleet, driving the other two
#
# Paths may be absolute or relative to the directory holding the script.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the credentials live. An encrypted sidecar at "${CREDS_FILE}.enc" is
# preferred automatically when present, so this path does NOT change when you
# encrypt the file - the scripts find the .enc on their own and fall back to
# the plaintext for as long as both exist.
#
#CREDS_FILE="${BASE_DIR}/creds/ilo_creds"

# The private key that decrypts the sidecar. Defaults to ~/.ssh/id_rsa, which
# is normally right - set this only when the service account's key lives
# somewhere else. It must be a classic PEM RSA key; ssh_crypt.sh refuses the
# modern OpenSSH container format and ED25519 keys up front.
#
#CREDS_SSH_KEY="${HOME}/.ssh/id_rsa"

# Credentials may also be set here directly, as an ordered list tried in the
# order written. Prefer the credentials file - anything in this file is
# plaintext on disk and this file is not the one that gets encrypted.
#
# There is no fleet default and no per-datacenter override: the list IS the
# whole statement of intent.
#
#ILO_CREDS=(
#  "0:username:password"
#  "1:username:password"
#)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What counts as a finding
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Report Warning findings as well as Critical.  (--warnings)
# Critical-only is the default because that is the list someone acts on today;
# warnings are real but rarely same-day.
#
#INCLUDE_WARNINGS=0

# Also report entries already flagged repaired in the IML.  (--include-repaired)
# Off by default: "repaired" is set by hand by whoever swapped the part, so an
# entry carrying it has already been dealt with.
#
# HPE ONLY. The Lenovo XCC has no repaired flag, so this does nothing there.
#
#INCLUDE_REPAIRED=0

# Which event categories count as hardware faults.  (--all-categories)
#
# HPE tags every IML entry: Hardware, Power, Administration, Configuration,
# Firmware, Security, Maintenance. That tag is the only reliable way to tell a
# failed part from an administrative notice - the firmware reports "Factory
# Root CA is about to expire" as CRITICAL, and "Time & Date Not Set" as a
# warning, and neither means any hardware has failed. Reporting those as
# hardware faults is how a fleet report becomes noise nobody reads.
#
# An entry is kept if it carries AT LEAST ONE of these categories, so a mixed
# tag like ["Security","Hardware"] is kept on the strength of Hardware.
#
# Lenovo supplies no categories; check_lenovo_iml.sh derives an equivalent
# from Oem.Lenovo.Source (see lib/lenovo_map.sh) so both vendors filter alike.
#
#HW_CATEGORIES="Hardware Power"
#ALL_CATEGORIES=0

# Age cutoff. BOTH ARE UNSET BY DEFAULT, deliberately.
#
# The IML never rotates, so a fault stays in it until someone marks it
# repaired. An unrepaired uncorrectable memory error is still a dead DIMM
# whether it was logged last night or last year, and a default window would
# quietly report such a host as healthy. Age is reported instead, in the
# FirstSeen / LastSeen / AgeDays columns, so you can filter downstream.
#
# Set one of these only when you specifically want recent activity - for
# instance a daily run that asks "what broke since yesterday".
#
#SINCE_DAYS=90                 # --days 90
#SINCE_DATE="2026-01-01"       # --since 2026-01-01


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# "csv" (default) or "report".  (--csv / --report)
# CSV is the machine format the fleet driver concatenates; its column names
# and order are a contract, so do not reorder them.
#
#OUTPUT_FORMAT="csv"

# Emit the CSV header line.  (--no-header)
# The fleet driver turns this off for the per-host runs it concatenates, and
# writes one header itself.
#
#PRINT_HEADER=1

# Write output here instead of stdout.  (--output FILE)
#
#OUTPUT_FILE=""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Naming and layout
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the hostlist/*.hosts and *.host-sps files live. Generated by
# do_lsf_generate_hostlist. The single-host scripts consult the .host-sps
# files to recognise a name they are handed; the fleet driver reads both.
#
#HOSTS_DIR="${BASE_DIR}/hostlist"

# Suffix appended to a bare host name to reach its service processor, used
# only when the host lists do not resolve the name. The six datacenters use
# three conventions (-sp, -ipmi, mp<host>), which is exactly why the lists are
# consulted first rather than this being assumed.
#
#SP_SUFFIX="-sp"

# Domain appended when a bare name does not resolve.
#
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY IN PRODUCTION. On atlvhwerptd01 /etc/resolv.conf carries
# "search amd.com", so bare host and SP names resolve natively and setting a
# suffix here is at best redundant. It exists for environments without that
# search path.
#
#HOST_SUFFIX=""

# Scratch space. Work directories are created here with mktemp -d and removed
# on exit, including on failure.
#
#TMP_DIR="${BASE_DIR}/tmp"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redfish transport
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#ILO_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=10
#ILO_MAX_TIME=60
#ILO_RETRIES=1

# Seconds to wait between credential attempts on one service processor.
#
# DO NOT LOWER THIS TO SPEED UP A SWEEP. After a couple of rejected logins an
# iLO answers 401 to every login for a short window, valid ones included. A
# credential list walked faster than that window locks itself out on the wrong
# passwords and then reports the CORRECT one as rejected, which is
# indistinguishable from a genuinely unknown credential.
#
# The effective pace is max(this, the SP's own published LoginFailureDelay),
# which the scripts read unauthenticated and free from /redfish/v1/. A host
# declaring 0 has still been observed exhibiting the lockout, so that published
# value is treated as a floor to respect, never as proof no delay applies.
#
#ILO_AUTH_RETRY_DELAY=15


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Learned credential map
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Remembers which credential worked for each service processor, so later runs
# go straight to it. This matters: the fleet is genuinely mixed - the Gen11
# hosts measured here take a different credential from the older ones - so an
# unaided run spends three failed logins and 45 seconds on every Gen11
# machine, every time. With the map that drops to one login and about four
# seconds. Measured on atlhwe-lsf309: 54s cold, 4s warm.
#
# ONLY THE LABEL IS STORED, NEVER A PASSWORD. Each file holds one line - the
# label from the ILO_CREDS entry that won. This is a guarantee: do not cache
# the credential itself, or this directory becomes a secret store that needs
# the same protection as creds/.
#
# Path mirrors inventory_script's production setting (INVENTORY_BASE/tmp/credmap).
#
#CREDMAP_DIR="${BASE_DIR}/tmp/credmap"
#USE_CREDMAP=1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hostlist regeneration from LSF
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The sweep rebuilds hostlist/ from LSF before enumerating it, mirroring
# run_inventory.sh's stage 1. A frozen hostlist drifts out of step with the
# fleet - hosts added since it was last written are never checked at all, and
# decommissioned ones are reported unreachable on every run. Set to 0, or pass
# --no-generate, on a machine with no LSF client or when deliberately sweeping
# a fixed list.
#
#DO_GENERATE=1

# Where do_lsf_generate_hostlist lives. It writes ./hostlist relative to its
# CWD, which is what lets the driver run it inside a staging directory and
# verify the output before promoting it over the live copy.
#
#GENERATE_HOSTLIST="${BASE_DIR}/do_lsf_generate_hostlist"

# Sourced so lshosts/bhosts can be verified present BEFORE generating.
# This is the single most important line in this section: the generator exits
# 0 having written EMPTY host lists when LSF is unreachable, so its exit status
# proves nothing and an unchecked run can wipe a good spine. On atlvhwerptd01
# lshosts/bhosts are not on a non-interactive SSH PATH; this profile adds them.
# Note the generator sources this same path itself.
#
#LSF_PROFILE="/tool/pandora/etc/lsf/profile.lsf"

# Datacenters a complete generation must produce. Each needs BOTH a <dc>.hosts
# and a <dc>.host-sps - a host present in the first but missing from the second
# is reported NO_SP_ENTRY rather than swept, so a half-generation would turn
# the whole fleet into NO_SP_ENTRY rows without ever looking unreachable.
#
#EXPECTED_DCS="atl caco dfdc pldc sadc syco"

# Plausibility floors a staged hostlist must clear before it replaces the live
# one. MIN_HOSTS is absolute; MIN_HOST_PERCENT is measured against the CURRENT
# list, which is what catches a partial LSF answer - non-empty, so it passes
# every other check, but far too small to be the real fleet. Set
# MIN_HOST_PERCENT=0 when the fleet genuinely shrinks below the threshold.
#
#MIN_HOSTS=100
#MIN_HOST_PERCENT=80


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lenovo
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which XCC log service to read. PlatformLog is the IML equivalent and the
# only one that carries severity-tagged hardware events - measured across both
# Lenovo hosts, ActiveLog and SaLog were empty, SEL returned 404, and
# MaintenanceLog and DiagnosticLog have no Severity field at all. AuditLog
# holds logins and config changes, not hardware faults, and is not read.
#
# NOTE: the XCC caps this log at 1024 entries and will not page past them, so
# on a busy machine older events are simply not retrievable over Redfish.
# check_lenovo_iml.sh warns when it sees exactly 1024.
#
#LENOVO_LOG="PlatformLog"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# APPENDED to PATH. The script sets its own hardened PATH up front
# (/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin) because cron
# and systemd supply almost nothing, then appends this.
#
# Appended, never prepended, and that matters more than it looks: a site
# directory like pandora64 often carries its own date, grep, sed, awk, sort,
# python3 and openssl at different versions from the system ones. Prepending
# swaps the whole toolchain underneath scripts written against the system
# copies. Append, and the directory supplies only what is genuinely absent -
# on atlvhwerptd01 that is jq alone, which is the one thing a run cannot
# proceed without.
#
#EXTRA_PATH="/tool/pandora64/bin"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Run tag and transcript
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Prefix for an AUTO-generated run tag: "prod-" yields prod-20261018-191106.
# Ignored when --run-tag is given, which is always taken verbatim.
#
# Setting this is what lets a scheduled job be just
#     check_hardware.sh --conf <conf> --fresh
# with no wrapper computing a timestamp and redirecting output by hand.
#
#RUN_TAG_PREFIX="prod-"

# Where each run writes its own transcript, as <run-tag>.log, with latest.log
# symlinked to the newest. Default ${TMP_DIR}/logs.
#
# Do NOT set RUN_TAG itself in a conf. A fixed tag is reused on every run, and
# re-using a tag RESUMES from the per-host cache - which has no age check - so
# a nightly job would re-publish its first night's findings forever while
# looking entirely healthy.
#
#LOG_DIR="${TMP_DIR}/logs"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Retention
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Prune LOCAL run artefacts older than this many days: reports/<tag>/,
# tmp/fleet/<tag>/ and tmp/logs/<tag>.log. 0 keeps everything. Default 90.
#
# The per-host cache under tmp/fleet/ dominates the footprint - roughly 8 MB a
# run against a 1600-host fleet, versus ~150 KB of report and a few KB of log.
#
# NOTHING under OUTPUT_DIR is pruned. OUTPUT_DIR is the PUBLISHED destination
# in this project, and a scheduled job must not delete files that other tools
# are reading. Retiring published CSVs is a deliberate manual act.
#
# Only directories containing this script's own output are removed, and the
# current run is always skipped - a wrong clock must not delete a finished
# sweep. Use --dry-run to see what a prune would do without doing it.
#
#RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Debug logging on stderr.  (-v)
#
#VERBOSE=0

# "auto" (colour only on a terminal), "always" or "never".
#
#LOG_COLOR="auto"
